# missing-referenced-file.tflw — CHECK-ONLY FIXTURE (M97c): a path literal naming a file that is
# not there — demonstrates `tflw check`'s teaching diagnostic (TF043: checker,
# `checkReferencedFiles`, tflw M97c / review `A4-07`).
#
# SPEC §4.3 has said "checker verifies the file exists" since M2.5 and it never did. The review row
# concluded the checker *could not*, because it does no I/O — a wrong conclusion from a true
# premise. The no-I/O rule belongs to the `@tflw/lang` package, so the docs-site editor demo can run
# the real checker in a browser; the `tflw check` *command* has read imported files from disk since
# M87. So the check was implementable all along, and what it needed was placement.
#
# What that cost, measured on the built CLI before M97c:
#
#   $ tflw check t.tflw          # import "./nowhere.tflw"
#   1 file checked, no problems found.
#   $ tflw run t.tflw --verbose
#     ✗ t.tflw (crashed) (0 ms)
#
# That is the *entire* console output of the run, `--verbose` included. The real message —
# `could not read imported file "./nowhere.tflw"` — reached only `report/results.json`. Existence
# checking removes the most common cause of that silent crash; it does not fix the swallowing,
# which is the report-honesty group's row and still open.
#
# Seven syntaxes open a file — `import`, `use`, `with each from`, `body from`, `upload`,
# `matches file`, `drop file` — and all seven are covered. Two are here; the rule is one pass over
# a table, not seven hand-written cases, so a third would test the table rather than the pass.
#
# The last test is *valid* and reports nothing, and it is the important one to read. `upload
# "./{name}.png"` names no file until the run picks a `name`, so the checker is silent: **not
# knowable is not known-bad**. Inverting that would trade a checker that misses errors for one that
# invents them, and a suite that legitimately builds fixture paths out of variables would stop
# checking. That is the same permissive half `matcher-subject-mismatch.tflw` pins open for TF042.
#
# Run explicitly (dot-directory, invalid syntax — see bad-keyword.tflw's header for why):
#   npx tflw check tests/.checkonly/missing-referenced-file.tflw

import "./nowhere.tflw"

test "reads a request body from a file that is not there"
  api POST /v1/orders body from "./payloads/nobody.json"
  expect status equals 201

test "an interpolated path is the runtime's question, not the checker's"
  let name = "receipt"
  api POST /v1/orders upload "./fixtures/{name}.png" as "file"
  expect status equals 201
